

1. git clone (showing how the GitHub repository is copied down to a local PC)



cd C:\GitHub
git clone https://github.com/pfizer-rd/cid-rapd-hamilton.git

Then:

cd cid-rapd-hamilton
dir

Show that the local PC now has the repo structure, for example:

PC-Environment
Method-Packages
LiquidClasses

Say:

> “This recreates the current repository contents on the local PC from GitHub.”



2. git status (showing the starting baseline before making any change)



git status

Ideally it shows:

nothing to commit, working tree clean

Say:

> “This tells us the local copy currently matches the Git-controlled baseline.”



3. git switch -c (showing how a proposed change can be isolated without changing the main baseline)



git switch -c demo-method-change

Say:

> “This creates a separate proposed-change workspace while leaving the main baseline untouched.”



4. Make your VENUS method change (showing a real controlled method update)



At this point, leave Command Prompt.

Open the development method in VENUS and make one small safe change.

Save the method normally through VENUS.

If VENUS edits files under:

C:\Program Files (x86)\Hamilton\Methods

copy the changed method files back into the repo under:

C:\GitHub\cid-rapd-hamilton\PC-Environment\Hamilton\Methods

5. Export a new method package (showing how the updated method results in a new controlled package version)



Export the updated package through the normal Hamilton package export process.

Put the new .pkg file under the appropriate repo location, for example:

C:\GitHub\cid-rapd-hamilton\Method-Packages\96\...

or:

C:\GitHub\cid-rapd-hamilton\Method-Packages\384\...

6. git status (showing Git automatically detects changed method files and the new package)



Back in Command Prompt:

cd C:\GitHub\cid-rapd-hamilton
git status

This is one of the most important demo moments.

Show that Git identifies things such as:

modified:
PC-Environment/Hamilton/Methods/...

new file:
Method-Packages/96/...

Say:

> “Git immediately identifies which controlled files changed and which new package was added.”



7. git diff (showing exact content differences where the file format is readable)



git diff

Use this to show line-level differences for readable text files such as .hsl or .sub, where applicable.

For a cleaner summary:

git diff --stat

Say:

> “For text-readable files we can see the exact content change. For binary files Git still records that the file changed.”



8. git add (showing which digital components are being proposed as part of the controlled change)



If you changed both method files and a package:

git add PC-Environment Method-Packages

Then:

git status

Now show that the files appear under:

Changes to be committed

Say:

> “These are now the exact files proposed as part of this change.”



9. git commit (creating the permanent technical audit record for that change)



Use a change-control-style message:

git commit -m "DEMO-CC-001 Update DemoMethod and export new package"

Say:

> “The commit records the change as one unit, including the author, timestamp, files affected, and a description that can reference the formal change-control record.”



10. git log (showing the audit history)



git log --oneline -5

Show the new commit in the history.

You can also show more detail:

git show --stat

Say:

> “Now the change is part of the technical history rather than just another copied file.”



11. git push (sending the proposed change back to GitHub without directly changing main)



git push -u origin demo-method-change

Say:

> “This sends the proposed change to GitHub for review while the main baseline remains unchanged.”



12. Create the Pull Request in GitHub (showing change review and change-control support)



In GitHub:

base: main
compare: demo-method-change

Use a title like:

DEMO-CC-001 - DemoMethod Update

In the description:

Change Control: DEMO-CC-001

Purpose:
Demonstrate controlled update of DemoMethod.

Expected changes:
- Modified method files
- New method package
- No unrelated library or dependency changes expected

Then show the Files changed tab.

Say:

> “This is the proposed digital change before it becomes part of the baseline.”



13. Show GitHub history (showing who changed what and when)



Click through:

Commits

Files changed

author

date/time

comments

approvals/checks


Say:

> “This gives us a traceable record of who made the change, when it happened, what files changed, and how it was reviewed.”



14. Merge the PR (only if you actually want the demo change incorporated into development main)



If you do not want this demo change retained, stop before merge.

If you do want it retained:

Merge pull request

Then locally:

git switch main
git pull origin main

Say:

> “Once approved and merged, the reviewed change becomes part of the new repository baseline.”



15. Optional rollback/history example (showing that previous versions remain recoverable)



To show history for a specific file:

git log -- PC-Environment\Hamilton\Methods\YourMethod\YourMethod.hsl

To inspect an earlier commit:

git show <commit-id>

You do not have to actually restore anything during the meeting. Just show that prior states remain available.

The simplest narration for the whole demo is:

git clone
(show how GitHub creates the local controlled copy)

git status
(show clean starting baseline)

git switch -c
(show isolated proposed change)

Make method change in VENUS
(show real workflow)

Export new package
(show package version update)

git status
(show exactly what Git detected)

git diff
(show exact readable changes)

git add
(select files belonging to the change)

git commit
(create the audit record)

git log
(show change history)

git push
(send proposed change to GitHub)

Pull Request
(show review/change-control evidence)

Merge
(make approved change the new baseline)

The commands I would literally keep open in a Notepad window for yourself during the demo are:

cd C:\GitHub

git clone https://github.com/pfizer-rd/cid-rapd-hamilton.git

cd cid-rapd-hamilton

git status

git switch -c demo-method-change

REM --- make VENUS change and export package here ---

git status

git diff

git diff --stat

git add PC-Environment Method-Packages

git status

git commit -m "DEMO-CC-001 Update DemoMethod and export new package"

git log --oneline -5

git show --stat

git push -u origin demo-method-change

For today's audience, I would focus most of your time on git status → git diff → commit → GitHub PR. That's where they will actually see the value rather than just hear that GitHub has version control.
